export const formattingExampleBody = [
  "A piece by artist collective Claire Fontaine entitled Untitled (Destroy and Rejuvenate) (2004) comprised of text printed on a match box summons Walter Benjamin’s scrutiny of the destructive character, an analysis nested within a much richer elaboration of messianic time in contradistinction to the “empty” homogenous time of capital. Under capitalism, life is worth only what the value form can procure from social time after having constituted itself on the dead labour of the living. Socially necessary labour time as contained in the commodity form is translated into money in a transactional moment in which that time is finds its valorized ends as surplus value in the form of currency, a transubstantiation of being that evacuates any lived experience into metricized abstraction. But in contradistinction to Benjamin’s poetic magical thinking, artist collective Claire Fontaine sets the materialist dimension of his project back on its feet. “A fire is a fire is not a fire,” draws on Benjamin’s writing on the “destructive character” only to set it into a conversation with that ubiquitous commodity of settler colonial capital: the cigarette packet. The text on the match box of Untitled (Destroy and rejuvenate) is made to be cut out and to slide under the plastic wrapping of a cigarette packet, its inferred twin object, which covered state health warnings such us “smoke damages is your health,” “smoke causes lung cancer,” and other such mandatory claims on the part of biopower (the state) which it denies its complicity in the empire of a global market that enables its power. The newly forged link between text and object replaces a hypocritically paternalizing message with the manifest fact that life destroys us anyway, all the more brutally in the instrumental grind of meeting a bottom line in which continual labour is rewarded by consumerism, entangling subject and object in a kind of symbiotic web of misery reflecting objective immiseration. As the lyrics go:",
  "When I'm drivin' in my car, and the man comes on the radio<br />He's tellin' me more and more about some useless information<br />Supposed to fire my imagination<br /><br />When I'm watchin' my TV and a man comes on and tells me<br />How white my shirts can be<br />But, he can't be a man 'cause he doesn't smoke<br />The same cigarettes as me<sup>1</sup>",
  "Here, it isn’t the things listed that fuel, “fire up,” or destroy and incinerate life and its potential but the entire world comprised of an entangled matrix of objects forming subjectivities that produce objects in a kind of infinitely mutually immiserating entwinement. While the cautionary messaging on mass commodities touch on any number of conscious concerns, it over writes the degree to which the object’s true destructive nature lies in its necessity to an equally destructive totality hostile to human life.",
  "How does all this unfold from a matchbook recoded as an artwork by a collective straddling the opaque hybrid object knows as the readymade as well as conceptual art’s opaque language? Art’s affordance, for which this essay argues, into the cultural unconscious – its transparency to that unconscious –renders it at first opaque. The work is opaque to the extent that it “represents” an abstract world wherein social synthesis itself is a function of the transactional logic of the market that elevates commodities over people excepting their capacity of bearers of the commodity labour power. This statement already dismantles and reconstitutes representation, a question to which this essay returns. Indeed, because – not despite--  the absence of an available way to depict totality, totality “poses an aesthetic problem, in the sense of demanding ways of representing the complex and dynamic relations intervening between domains of production, consumption and distribution, of making the invisible visible.”<sup>2</sup>Its very enigmatic character nonetheless turns on itself to signal and probes the way in which abstraction is ultimately not a quality of culture but of the social field, or second nature under capital. It inverts appearances to takes seriously the suggestion that “activities of modelling, diagramming and envisioning are thus representational in what is perhaps a counter intuitive sense, since they break with a model of representation as mirror, photograph or correlation between signifier and signified, index and referent. As representations of abstract processes and relations, they are also representations of invisibilities.”<sup>3</sup> The work of art is a form of modelling, diagramming and envisioning that is “abstract” only to the extent that the world is abstract as a function of market transactions that resonate in and reconstitute social relations. Because the work affords a moment of “transparent” access to the social economic field of the real and the totality of social forms it synthesizes, it appears at first all the more opaque. The theory of art that this essay forwards is thus very reverse of the theory of the autonomy of art which situates the culture work as an opaque expression of a graspable and figurable totality. But it also argues against the common notion of the culture work as a fragment in an accessible social totality. As I argue, the work of art as an index of an abstract totality defines that totality.",
  "The art work presses into territory only barely circumscribed by denotative discourse. A social unconscious understood as a form of real abstraction has otherwise been described as the “scandalous character of the Freudian unconscious.” Slavoj Zizek, reading psychoanalytic theory within and against Marxism, sees this “open secret of real abstraction,” which results from exchangism as the only fully shared social process.  The transaction that realizes social time in currency, the only form that connects all to all and that which alone synthesizes the social field despite its autonomy from social life does so in a way that happens unconsciously insofar as it is suspended from the rest of lived experience.<sup>4</sup> “Real abstraction is the unconscious of the transcendental subject.”<sup>5</sup> This empirically real yet unconscious abstraction undergirding social relations forges the subject before the subject can even “think” its reach. For Zizek and others, abstraction in social relations not only precedes but is the primary causal factor of abstract thought, effectively reversing the lines of cause and effect through which the word “abstraction” is most always deployed.  Commonly, and in an internally differentiated and complex history that might begin with Plato and Aristotle, abstraction is understood to be a function of thinking, which is then projected onto the empirical world.  Reversing this derivation of abstraction to replace the primacy of the concept with that of practice, Zizek relies on Alfred Sohn-Rethel who claims, “A derivation of consciousness from social being presupposes a process of abstraction which is part of this being.”<sup>6</sup>  Abstraction, then, originates not in “men’s minds but in their actions. And yet this does not give ‘abstraction’ a merely metaphorical meaning.  It is abstraction in its precise, literal sense.” Abstraction derives ironically from the fact that “the consciousness of those involved in it is blind to the socially synthetic character of the actions.”<sup>7</sup> That socially synthetic character derives from the market value of labour time is the very matrix of a totalization of all and every aspect of life that becomes second nature, naturalized to the point of invisibility. Again, this second nature, or totality, operates beneath individually or collectively conscious social forms that could think or grasp this totality in any formalizable way – with the exception of the possibilities of consciousness precipitated by the artwork. This exception aside, categories born of abstract thought drive thinking a social synthesis so fully, absolutely and unconsciously reticulated to market dynamics as to become themselves all but unthinkable.",
  "While the consciousness of those involved in exchange plays a role as their private consciousness, as isolated subjectivity, their actions remain objectively social, though unconsciously abstract and non-empirical. What conceals the abstractness of their activity from those involved in exchange is precisely their consciousness. The sensuous perception of the empirical qualities of things through the eyes, the ears, the sense of touch, etc. As against this, the value-relation of the products of labor within which it appears, have absolutely no connection with the physical nature of the commodity and the material [dinglich] relations arising out of this. The abstractness of exchange thus applies to the activity and precisely not to the thoughts and consciousness of those involved. <sup>8</sup><br />",
  "Sohn-Rethel swivels the problem of “abstraction” away from a “thought abstraction,” or the cognitive operations that Althusser – and for that matter the history of western philosophy and epistemology, and back on the objective processes that transpire at the market, in the act of exchange.",
  "Totality, for Sohn-Rethel and Zizek, is thus defined otherwise to historical models of totalitarian social or political structure.  It is that which is already silently and invisibly – because unconsciously—empirically operative, as generalized as capital itself, but forges this synthesis outside of discourse. This model of a shared social unconscious generated of real transactions and yet fully occluded could not be more radically different than the classical model of the individual unconscious. But art’s affordances and access to this space, rather than the unconscious itself, preoccupy this paper.",
  "In Zizek’s Sohn-Rethelian account of abstraction, social synthesis happens not only unconsciously, but is reticulated to a form of action enacted on a market.  This action is the exchange.  This moment through which labour time is commensurate with money, with surplus value, is as indifferent to the thoughts of the individuals involved as to the qualities of the objects exchanged.  It becomes a form akin to a technology of the social in which an operation determines movement across production, consumption and circulation in broader social circuits that ultimately subsume all of social life—as a silent totality.<sup>9</sup> Any elision or slide from intention or will to blind operation is structural to the act: “In an exchange society based on commodity production, however, the social power has lost its personal character and in its place is an anonymous necessity...and has assumed the compelling necessity of an objective social law.”<sup>10</sup> It is produced by human actions at the limit of habit, “behind the backs” of agents, according to its own balance sheet in a way that crosses identity and difference, clan, tribe, society, etc.  This structural nature renders each transaction anonymous despite the social identifications attributable to actants. Here, ethics, like agency, are sidelined not through the maliciousness of the actors but in the indifferent necessity of exchange itself and through an accumulation of exchanges in which the autonomy of the value form are to be guaranteed over and above any lived contingency. To put it another way, then, totality is itself a function of abstraction. But this seems to again run in a circular logic. How to articulate the way in which this transactional operation comes to saturate or “totalize,” invisibly?",
  "But what then is “real abstraction” here if not just another term for reification, for the ontological shifts in everyday life brought about by the triumvirate of wage, anonymous labor, and the commodity?<sup>11</sup> Reification, a problem elaborated at length by Gyorgy Lukacs and haunting the history of Twentieth Century art, is defined by political economist Anita Chari as “a depoliticizing form of consciousness that mis recognizes the practical basis of the individuals own activity and its role in constituting the social world.”<sup>12</sup> For Chari artwork walks a fine line in dialectical relation to the reified object, a position affording it the capacity to either spark off a moment of de-reification or slide back into regular naturalized vision.<sup>13</sup> But while Chari is concerned with the capacity of the object of art to dereify, this essay is interested in the way in which that interval afforded by the dereifying art work offers access to an otherwise unfigurable  social totality insofar as it mediates the social unconscious, or the market, at the level of culture. Chari also takes artist collective Claire Fontaine’s work as exceptional in its dereifying function. In her account of her their piece entitled America Bunt/Unburnt, an installation involving thousands of match sticks arranged into the shape of the American Flag on view at gallery in the San Francisco Bay area in the United States in 2013, Chari walks the reader through a mental conversation that ends with the conclusion: “private property= art work = America.”<sup>14</sup>  For Chari this conceit-as-art acts as a vanishing rare site to locate a diagnosis of a social, political economic horizon in a swift, succinct almost immediate and intuitive way.",
  "My intervention here is to elaborate Chari’s account of not only how Claire Fontaine offer a diagnosis of social synthesis, and why contemporary aesthetic practices uniquely afford access to form of totality, but above all, how they redefine totality as such by probing it.  This necessitates a review of the massive extant literature on totality in relation to culture. But first, I turn to Claire Fontaine’s writing on abstraction for the way in which they frame it as the dominating form of social totality made all the more urgent by its invisibility.  Part of what I hope to show is that contemporary art’s preoccupation with abstraction as a function of social relations that appear fragmented and atomized but which are determined by intangible coherence is the very symptom of that totality as it generates a scrambled etiology.  In the blind field produced of that imperceptible abstracting totality’s, forms of thought and action that de reify and recode thought and action as such are singularly able to advance a means of conscious vision.",
  "Claire Fontaine, like Zizek, define abstraction through a reading of Intellectual and Manual Labour.  Almost a decade prior to recent retranslations of Sohn Rethel’s work and the attendant discussions to which recent retranslations have given rise, already in 2013 Claire Fontaine had identified Intellectual and Manual Labour as a singular source for any understanding of abstraction.<sup>15</sup> They take this text up in “Existential Metonymy and Imperceptible Abstractions” to precipitate the question of a hidden social synthesis, or rather that totality is everywhere and nowhere, accessible consciously only as fetish and fragment, yet reticulated to a network caused by exchange. Claire Fontaine defined her understanding of abstraction as a function of “exchangism.” They explicitly cite Sohn-Rethel’s theorization of a market unconscious offering a sole, if invisible, form of unity in an otherwise seemingly fragmented and separated global social order. They point to the asymmetrical indifference of the act of transactional in the market for which every object is destined and around which every subjective experience revolves, as the source of in a unfigurable  because  “imperceptible” totality.<sup>16</sup>  “These social relations are imperceptible abstraction.”<sup>17</sup> They note that abstraction is elusive because as an effect of social relations saturated under capital, everywhere and locatable nowhere in particular, an empirical effect of political economy rather than a question of thought.<sup>18</sup>   That Sohn Rethel’s work has come to resonate not only in debates in political economy but also art and art history in an aesthetic tradition that long jettisoned figuration, identification or representation for other forms, might begin to suggest something about the un/figurability of “totality.” That Claire Fontaine express interest in real abstraction as a function of occluded social synthesis already introduces the problem of totality and the need to redefine it.",
  "But how might the perception of fragmentation be itself a function of another kind of totality, a form of totalitarianism presenting as a blind field in pieces? Capital is oriented exclusively to the law of value, over and against human experience much less consciousness. This is in and of itself an inhuman drive to totality. In this sense, despite the appearance of fragmentation, separation and reification demanded by the capitalist mode of production, the scene of valorization is itself a synthesis. If all human striving is reticulated to the law of value by which human life is daily reproduced, and to the market as the site at which value is valorized as such, only the market as this site becomes itself a “universal” space. At the same time, insofar as all other social relations are momentarily suspended in the transactional logic of this universal space, insofar as the exchange itself is indifferent to all other social forms outside of valorization, the totalizing force of exchange comes to be both omnipresent and yet utterly missed -- a missed recognition par excellence which comes to structure all other forms of mis/ recognition. Part of this mis recognition is due to a long reified division between intellectual and manual labour.",
  "Part of the perceptual conundrum derives from the separation of intellectual and manual labour and the faculties demanded of each.  The division cleaves human activity into reflective activities and into manual production (agrarian, industrial, etc.) each of which require a different vector of mediated cognition with relation to the product.  This primary separation is both necessitated by a market and conducive to the formation of conceptual categories that are themselves reified products and that make even conceiving of the totality impossible. The forms of separation and division necessitated by the commodity labour power makes thinking about social relations outside the framework of the commodity labour power absurdist. Thought itself cannot think outside this second nature.",
  "Claire Fontaine’s makes a project of refusing the gap between intellectual and manual labour.  That project sets into motion the reversal of reification and process of defetishization to initiate a way of reconciling the separation of faculties necessary to market abstraction. Neither sculpture nor installation nor purely conceptual, work by Claire Fontaine such as Destroy and Rejuvenate or America Burnt Unburnt join their signature brickbats to unmake the division between action and thought. Bricks covered by book jackets of key theoretical interventions of the last century, from Marx to Carla Lonzi through Guy Debord,  tease the false distinction between analytical intellectual “work” and the dynamic of potential direct implied in picking up a book that doubles as a projectile in the scene of collective protest.  Claire Fontaine’s numerous brickbats defy categorization or medium, but also cancel and exceeds the false binary between theory and praxis.<sup>19</sup>",
  "Now, what has culture to do with this definition of abstraction as a concrete quality of everyday life and as a function of an unconscious social synthesis that takes “place” --although it suspends the particularity of both space and time—in exchange, at market, forming a “second nature?”  What is it about this renaturalization at an unconscious register, tethering all of social life behind the seeming scenes, that becomes a cultural issue when it is far from resolved in political economy? The debate in value form theory and other areas of Marxology suggests that the status of the market in relationship to the capitalist mode of production measured  in socially necessary labour time is debatable at best, if not controversial. Marx, after all, began with use-values and with the process of value production before proceeding to an analysis of exchange; however, the circuits of production and circulation (and consumption) are shown to be mutually causative suggestive of the co constitutive relationship of the market and sites of production by the mid 20th century. The problem of social subsumption will arise later in the essay. For us, what matters is the issue of perception and articulation, those concerns that preoccupy culture. Sohn-Rethel’s insistence that market exchangism generates thought categories prior to capitalist production, a development that produced both knowledge and the frameworks through which to think the empirical world become part of the problem of grasping social synthesis at the level of consciousness rather than the nature of production as such. The occlusion of a way in which to production as the acceleration of  the sale of labour time already posed a problem for Marx.",
  "And yet in the culture sector, and contemporary art in particular, this seemingly obscure issue germane to debates in Marxology has been precipitated in advance by artists like Claire Fontaine precisely for the way in which it affords a contrivance by which to see the seemingly dispersed social register as a total social picture. Claire Fontaine remark with astonishment that one procedure, the market valorization of value in the moment of exchange, could determine all social relations elsewhere, off market site: “One could even go so far as to formulate the hypothesis that, just as the value-form contaminates the whole realm of objects, including those that aren’t commodities, in the same way this value form injects exchange value into behaviors, colonizes or infects all human behaviors including the most spontaneous, emotional and disinterested ones.”<sup>20</sup> Here, “exchangism,” or the market transaction where goods and labour time are valorized, comes to determine any and all social relations which seem independent of the market in any seemingly empirical way.",
  "Occasionally, a scholarly piece will surface that will assert this unity.<sup>21</sup> Nicholas Green, for instance, has explored impressionist painting as a synthesis of transitions in the relationship between the state and the market in the 19th c in Paris, in which the latter gained prominence over the state which had had its own forms of dispensation to artists mediated by the institution of the salon.  Green notes that “the emergence of modern art dealing is a particular subdomain of capitalism, but one which sloughed off its artisanal skin by integrating financial strategies with a cultural repertoire.”<sup>22</sup> For Green, the formal experiments at stake in new painterly methods are part of a unity with new social formations made possible by the ascendence of the market.  He goes so far as to say that those preoccupied with political economy might do well to look to the culture sector for the degree to which the culture product can offer a way to see, because mediated, a set of relations in the social economic register otherwise inaccessible, because everywhere. <br /><br />How to map this everywhere? In Cartographies of the Absolute, Alberto Toscano and Jeffrey Kinkle pose a compelling question, long overdue, against the grain of recent decades of hegemonic discourses that have privileged the fragment, and the subjective, over any attempt to totalize.  They ask: why is totality, that which the call for this issue of Qui Parle identifies as a “crucial conceptual ground for understanding the relationship between particular cultural phenomenon and the social, political and economic forms that render them plausible,” impossible to perceive much less conceive and convey in any coherent form?<sup>23</sup> To that question, an addendum might be added. Why is any attempt to relate culture to social formations that are the very conditions of its possibility reduced to contextual reflection, even after decades of sophisticated cultural interpretive discourse? <sup>24</sup> For some, the danger is to press culture into the service of illustration, or to force it instrumentally to answer questions it would rather raise, despite the fact that even those voices most vociferously yoked to the specter of art’s long dismissed autonomy, such as Theodor Adorno, explicitly stated that “there is no material content, no formal category of artistic creation, however mysteriously changed and unknown to itself, which did not originate from the empirical reality from which it breaks,” reminding us that so called autonomy was not only ever provisional, but also nested in a “totality,” an index of a historical horizon.<sup>25</sup> The horizon itself – that evanescent totality was always at stake however much the cultural work might remain irreducible or opaque to it.  As Adorno noted, the “mysterious transmission” which the artist or culture worker generates may not be transparent or legible to that producer themselves, but nonetheless remains part of an empirical reality, however il/legible. And for his part, Clement Greenberg openly avowed the umbilical cord of gold that tethered artists to the social system via the patron and the institution.<sup>26</sup> Key here was the way in which the work could be possible despite, not because of, its inscription from and back into “gold” in the circuits of reception, circulation and production. For others, the nature of mediation between culture and social formation is too complicated, caught in a tangle of causality unable to be located – absent cause as argued by Jameson after Althusser. I will return to both.",
  "Notwithstanding the complex nature of mediation that these writers sought to describe in manifest resistance to any facile model of reflection, the result of their conceptualization was to make of the art work a fetish not only in the Marxian sense  – a work of art is not quite the same thing as a standard commodity fetish, but autonomy as such comes to operate as fetish – but also in the sense posited by Freud, as a mis understood part taken to ward off and deny the whole or a totality comprised of social relations too difficult to grasp or to bear.  Here, the work becomes a fetish  in the sense of the first usages of the term fetish by colonial settlers confronting new territories, as a device within material practice to posit the sacred, or a world beyond the secular.<sup>27</sup> As Walter Benjamin notes, even within a fully secularized framework, art work and culture has never fully shaken its last vestige of mythological use-value. But this archaic vestigial use-value as fetish wards off access to totality activated by works that refuse fetishization precisely to summon a sense of a field of synthesis under separation.",
  "In acknowledgement of the fraught territory into which they step, Toscano’s and Kinkle’s question rests on an earlier formulation, which they cite, by Frederic Jameson ,who defines totality as “what organizes history but is unrepresentable within it.”<sup>28</sup> But for the authors, as putatively unfigurable as totality may be understood as a political economic order undergirding any cultural logic, a drive to cultural forms of symbolization nonetheless persists. Correlation between political economy and culture may be impossible, rendering any form of reflective presentation moot, yet “each epoch develops cultural forms and modes of expression that allow it, however partially and ideologically, to represent its world –to totalize it.”<sup>29</sup> In this last statement, representation is understood as a [failed] desire to totalize. <br /><br />The question is posed most succinctly by Jameson in The Political Unconscious: Narrative as a Socially Symbolic Act and again in Postmodernism, or The Cultural Logic of Late Capitalism who asks why “totality” comes to be both the horizon of cultural mediation and the rock against which it is inevitability ship wrecked. Why has a social purview come to be invisible and ultimately absolutely unrepresentable to begin with? The easy answer is to fall back on the particularism induced by market synthesis as the very form of its own nefarious self-mis representation and to conclude that that any attempt to grasp the enormous complexity of life within global capital, much less present it, would exceed the consciousness of any one subject position situated within it. <sup>30</sup> Again, contingency cancels any total “view.” This answer may or may not be especially satisfying, as we will see in the essay to follow. However, it is one bid for why “realism” as a direct presentation of social reality is ultimately foreclosed within modernity if not within traditional culture; capital itself stands in excess to any access to the reality of itself, making of the social field a blind field to any one subject position inscribed within it. As Toscano and Kinkle note, attempts at social realism become partial perspectives of the social which, as illustrations from a contingent perspective, belies the point of realism. A circular logic results, ending in aporia or worse, the formation of particularistic ideology.  “”Ideology [is] the subject’s imaginary representation of their relation to the real.”<sup>31</sup>  Any figuration claiming to realism as such becomes a conditional imaginary, fetishized, and, therefore, merely symptomatic of the impossibility of totality.  The fragment resurfaces as the perceived “truth” of the situation, which becomes in and of itself a theory to account for the subject’s limited relation to a restricted totality.",
  "Jameson relies on Louis Althusser’s Marxian Lacanian understanding of subject formation, and of the individual’s partial relation to the social economic mediated by language.  Althusser read culture, and art in particular, as a path into the question of subjective misrecognition and mis identification. He saw it as an “illustration” of his own theory of the subject as a function of interpellation. Althusser’s dependence on the Lacanian understanding of the signifier in relation to a foreclosed real, which Althusser conceived specifically as the field of the social economic field, in turn afforded Frederic Jameson the theoretical apparatus through which to read culture as an etiology, or a repository of symptoms and clues, to an “absent cause” that could not be figured directly.  Jameson in turn elaborated a theory of hermeneutics, that of effectively replacing a “reflective” understanding of art with one wherein culture is the complex mediation of withdrawn causality issuing from a social field yet imperceptible within it.",
  "But again,  for both, the problem of unrepresentability remains on the side of the subject who, caught in a labyrinth of misprision, somewhere among the [social]real, imaginary and symbolic, at once interpellated into social formations and yet mis identified, is tasked pathetically with trying to tie a signifier onto a n opaque political economic reality in partial and structurally problematic ways. In the “blind field” of everyday life, as opposed to of film in relation to which Roland Barthes coined the term, the subject may as well be blind folded, groping to “read” the signs and sequences of the social order that in turn constitutes “the real.”<sup>32</sup> Much of the discourse of the last several decades that attempts to forge any convincing account of the relationship between the subject and the putative “real” passes through the theory of the subject offered by Lacan and relies therefore on a model of the unconscious also posed by him. While for Althusser, and therefore Jameson after him, this real is itself inscribed in the social political field, it is nonetheless illegible as a totality because it is sifted through the contingency of the subject’s partial purchase on the world outside of them.",
  "But there is also another, quite different, theory of the impossibility of totality. It rests on an analysis of objective conditions (rather than subjective contingency) within capital, one which posits the source of inevitably failed totalization within the nature of social relations wrought by capital itself regardless of the problem of any subjective purchase on it, although it too ultimately concludes with a theory of consciousness. Gyorgy Lukacs took the objective character of fragmentation and social separation required by and generated of the nature of capitalist accumulation – anonymous abstract labour time -- and the social forms it motivated to be the primary obstacle to class consciousness, indeed any consciousness of a social totality. For Lukacs, the forms of separation, anonymity, and other new social experiences demanded of compulsory proletarianization, combined with reification and attendant forms of appearance in capitalist everyday life made, any access to totality, understood in an organic or traditional sense, impossible. Any claim to a total view would be naïve at best and reactionary at worst precisely because totality had replaced any socially organic whole, again through its forms of compulsory separation, fragmentation and abstraction. An organic social whole had already been replaced by capital with a totality constituted of shattered bits of social de and re composition.   Lukacs had initially written in defence of traditional realist literature in his The Theory of the Novel in 1915. However, by 1938, in an essay entitled “Realism in the Balance,” Lukacs published in Das Wort, Lukacs was eager to retract his position, acknowledging that any attempt at realism as it was once understood was not only impossible but dangerously reactionary : “The objective product, The Theory of the Novel was a reactionary work in every respect, full of idealistic mysticism and false in all its assessments of the historical process.” <sup>33</sup> He then situates History and Class Consciousness written in 1920 as the “objective product” of his awareness of the initial mistake made in The Theory of the Novel. The latter text is a theorization of the wedge driven between experience and consciousness of it by capitalist social formations over writing any previous notion of “nature.”",
  "The economic relations that directly determined the metabolic relations between man and nature progressively disappear. Man becomes in the true sense of the word a social being. Society becomes the reality for man. Thus, the recognition that society is reality becomes possible only under capitalism in bourgeois society. But the class which carried out this revolution did so without consciousness of its function; the social forces it unleashed, the very forces that carried it to supremacy seemed to be opposed to it like a second nature but a more soulless, impenetrable nature than feudalism ever was.<sup>34</sup>",
  "The second nature produced by labour within capital develops against labor’s interest, and cannot be grasped by labour.",
  "So far, this essay has attempted to locate other models of probing social synthesis and abstraction to arrive at a model of cultural mediation that is neither reflective, nor the Althusserian model of ideological formation issuing from partial interpellation on which many Marxian cultural theories, above all Jameson’s, rests. The question of subjective mis recognition that became part of the enigma of cause and consequence within capital may indeed inhere, but there is another account, one which returns to objective conditions within capital from which issues a social synthesis causal of “totality,” but which also operates as a better explanation of the seeming opacity within which the subject is inscribed. This is to say that there is another way to locate the particular form of social synthesis within capital-as-second-nature that forges its own form of totality and which demands another explanation: abstraction. And here, aesthetic abstraction becomes a rebus through which to tease the open secret of real abstraction out of its dissembling secrecy.",
  "<br />There is also the urgent matter of why the very question of totality may be problematic if not outright offensive, regardless of any subjective inaccessibility to it. In the 21st century, any political assumptions or aspirations that might motivate an attempt at grasping “totality” is already suspect, given the fact that the term had for a long time come to be associated with 20th century forms of totalitarianism. “Totality in the ears of many still echoes with totalitarianism at worst or paranoid criticism at best…a category corrupted by the metaphysical desire for coherence and the hubris of intellectual mastery.” <sup>35</sup> And so,  even while “capitalist totality is devoid of any easily grasped command and control centre,”  attempts to posit one are perceived as corrupt.",
  "Yet for many, the very dissolution of any real threat of political totalitarianism, coded leftist, on a global stage made of capital the actual and prevailing totalitarianism. In Capitalist Realism, Mark Fisher takes up the problem of “realism” again, noting that unlike the traditional historical realisms foreclosed by modernism’s attempts to convey a fragmentary modernity, after 1989 an inverted realism becomes the only available cultural form insofar as capital becomes the sole global operational logic, re writing everything into which it comes into contact and thereby constituting a new real that only it can describe.<sup>36</sup> Because capital dominates, having gained world sovereignty, for Fisher only it succeeds in fully structuring popular culture. Popular culture, in turn, comes to reflect this newly constituted real wrought by capitalist production and circulation. In other words, all of culture comes to be subsumed into the expanded field of value production and realization. There is no outside and no “other” to capital from within.",
  "This much theorized foreclosure of individual or collective access to totality does not mean that an autonomous form of totality itself withdraws.  If anything, it expands through accelerating circuits of value. O the contrary, the problem with this conclusion in anxiety of thinking totality is that it cedes its terms to capital itself. Capital alone totalizes. It does so as the only means by which to replicate itself, through the circuits of accumulation that draw more resources into its cycle.  It accomplishes this totalitarian through subsumption. <sup>37</sup>  On a temporal level, this happens through expanding and differentiating forms of labour time; spatially through the appropriation of territory for the purposes of resource and labour extraction or to establish value productive facilities. These necessities compel the recomposition of class relations globally, which in turn deepens and widens the logic of the market. The latter reconstitutes social relations as transactions in a dispersed, estranged and competitive social ecology.",
  "<br />Another way of formulating this subjectively unfathomable objective condition whereby capital itself elaborates a market totalitarianism far from the feared manifestation of political totalitarianism , is to understand not only the problem of fragmentation at the level of appearances but rather to pivot the purview and to hone in on the way in which the sole synthesis of otherwise fragmented social relations takes place in the one site which most occludes the appearance of the social: the market.  This other formulation offers another path into and through any situated and fragmentary perception, now set into relation with a real synthesis, a place where fragmentation is drawn into a totality, but wherein that totalizing vanishing point takes place “behind the backs of men” forming a second nature.  This second nature is not unlike that posited by Lukacs, but might be located. Indeed, it is a reality that appears abstract only insofar as the reality of everyday life is made abstract by it.",
  "The market is the place where, through exchangism, all circuits of value ultimately connect; but which is missed through exchangism itself. At the level of subjective consciousness, the content and concrete qualities of the objects and services sold and bought over-writes the nature of necessary compulsions under capital, making the moment of exchange seem practical and fleeting, as though it had no bearing on any or all moments outside the transaction itself. However, beneath this subjective consciousness, the operations of the value form, of valorization, impose the anonymity and indifference of abstraction that comes to structure the rest of social life, concretely absent from the scene at market. While Toscano and Kinkle note the cartographic and diagrammatic impulse in culture to try to picture this totality. I turn to art work that demonstrates the circuits of that totality through strategies of that dismantle and blast through the opacity wrought by an abstract social field. This process of dereification mobilized by Claire Fontaine begins with the refusal to accept a division between manual and intellectual labor, followed by a careful analysis of how the subject is abstracted through transactional and exchangist actions that suspend consciousness. A such, they begin to generate a response to the central question: “How might we reclaim totality as a conceptual ground that can account for the relationship between experience, on the one hand, and historical-material processes that shape culture production on the one hand?”",
  "For Claire Fontaine, then,  Intellectual and Manual Labour provides a way to see the extent to which the twinned logic of value and exchange not only  effect social relations, but invisibly unify the seeming entropy in absolutist reticulation to a single operation on a market, a unifying operation all the more complete for its indiscernible nature. They attribute to Sohn-Rethel a conceptual way in to this “absurdity” wherein collective self-interest is negated by unconscious individual self-interest that ultimately also negates the subject. The market totalizes, creating not so much a political unconscious as an economic one that quietly tethers all of life to its interests, autonomously of human conscious or will.",
  "And this logical “absurdity,” again, results from value’s indifference to lived social relations, and the market’s orientation to the law of value in the moment of exchange, which forms a structural contradiction that structures all of everyday life.  The autonomy of value and indifference of the market might be co constitutive, but ramify as crisis in a social dimension that continues to be organized around family, community and social norms. The contradiction between this indifference and its contingent place in the social field form a faultline, each affective aspect resting in the shadow of the other.  The art work as a form of archaic use value as a form of social mediation and also as exhibitionary exchange value momentarily suspended in its presentation to the viewer, poses a problem. For while the art work is “purposeless” in any instrumental sense, its purposelessness dialectically summons the over determined sense of instrumental purpose of the commodity which, in the context of this specific art work, is shown to be the object of actual social indifference despite its obvious utility and price. The work of art that invites this degree of analysis of market exchangism places itself in asymmetrical relation to the double suspension of any personal interest in market exchangism in the ultimately interest of the indifferent and autonomous value form.",
  "<br />In conclusive sum, the way in which capital imperceptibly totalizes social relations also come to divide and disperse it at the level of conscious lived reality. It negates that which it re constitutes in its image. And this is why it is of note to artists, as a question of what is visible, perceptible, able to be conveyed: “the paradox is that commercial exchange is in reality a social tie created by an activity that negates it. Because the use of money allows authorizes every owner of commodities to abstractly (but concretely) pursue his own personal interests, without obstructions, without even thinking about his place in society.”<sup>38</sup> The space of transaction is anonymous, impersonal, obdurate and inflexible to experiential life much less consciousness of it. It forges an unconscious matrix impervious to will and reflective determination. As such, the transaction cancels community and replaces it with a capitalist unconscious. To emphasize: this unconscious space is not that theorized by psychoanalysis. It is simply that which motivates social relations under capital despite any individual free will or intention.",
  "And for any illumination into an undetectable social synthesis that occurs precisely at the very moment that it is least “viable,” namely a synthesis that is structurally hostile to social relations themselves, to surface, object and subject relations as they are have to be momentarily called to pause. And this pause or moment of suspension, also at the register of purpose against unconscious indifference, is what the art work accomplishes, This moment can open onto a de and recoding of appearances and ontologies. The seeming enigma of the work that poetically summons these concerns is the pause necessary to decoding.  But here again, the artist’s writing opens an interstice between political economy and aesthetics to mitigate the apparent opacity of the object which ultimately affords a kind of prism through which to see the abstraction of everyday life.  Again, the opacity of the art object is the only means into the opacity of the social field.",
] as const;

